documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:17:| 1 | **Methodology** — no single formal research question | Explain the origin and design of the project, why chess is used, what approaches worked or failed, how the computational design was made feasible, and how earlier components can be tested by others. | Connectome-derived constraints and the biological features retained or simplified. | Sparse graph simulation, architecture, implementation decisions, reproducibility, resource profiling, and reusable components. | Graph theory; sparse linear algebra; computational complexity; memory and runtime measurement. |
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:18:| 2 | **The Classroom** — *Can a simple organism-derived model based on a Drosophila connectome learn under computational constraints?* | Test whether the model learns from experience and feedback, with appropriate controls. | Learning, plasticity, feedback pathways, and the distinction between model learning and learning in a living fly. | Online learning, feedback delivery, experiment controls, and reproducible training records. | Dynamical systems for changing activity or memory; statistics for learning curves and control comparisons. |
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:20:| 4 | **The Limits of Biology** — *To what extent do memory and learning constraints limit performance in this model?* | Measure specific limits such as retention, forgetting, interference, capacity, and resource dependence. | Memory, forgetting, interference, retention, and biological plausibility of the modeled mechanisms. | Model capacity, resource bounds, failure analysis, and sensitivity to design assumptions. | Information theory and capacity; retention/decay models; uncertainty around measured limits. |
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:58:### Volume 3 — Reasoning
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:60:**Researcher clarification, 2026-11-01:** this is the volume for pushing the limits of biology and computer science to make the fly-connectome-derived system reason under computational constraints. Engineered memory and disclosed computational aids are permitted; an exact biological memory solution is not a prerequisite for that condition. State each component's contribution and test unfamiliar combinations and counterfactuals against matched retrieval-only/no-memory controls. The current engineered-memory direction is an enabling method for this volume, while biological memory-limit findings also inform Volume 4. [Volume 3 writing scope](../../../research/literature/writings/03-reasoning/README.md).
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:73:**Research question:** *To what extent do memory and learning constraints limit performance in this model?*
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:76:2. **How memory is represented** — Explain the modeled memory state, overlap, persistence, and biological assumptions.
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:96:- Volume 3 can provide evidence of transfer under the project's predefined tests. Chess transfer alone does not establish broad, cross-domain reasoning or reasoning in a living fly.
documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md:104:The volumes are not assumed to be five successive proof steps. In particular, chess strength in Volume 5 is a separate performance outcome from the transfer evidence in Volume 3, and the limits investigated in Volume 4 are not a pre-decided conclusion that reasoning is impossible.
research/project/decision_log.md:21:| 2026-09-29 | Require from-scratch learning of position evaluation; exclude pretrained chess weights. | Explicit researcher instruction; target generalization to unfamiliar positions. Reference-model memorization remains a hypothesis to test. | Fine-tuning an existing chess checkpoint. | Researcher explicitly specified initialization and objective. | `design/low_memory_training.md` |
research/project/decision_log.md:23:| 2026-10-07 | Measure and apply the first exact sparse-forward latency optimization before increasing training volume. | A precomputed derived signed-magnitude cache reduced one matched 53-presentation adaptive cycle from 60.25 s to 45.66 s (24.2% wall, 22.0% CPU). Selected move, certificate, persistent memory and all-legal ranking matched exactly. This is one-fixture engineering evidence; useful learning, Monte Carlo speed and reasoning remain unproved. Internal memory mutation paths refresh the cache; external direct magnitude mutation requires `setMagnitude`/`refreshSignedMagnitude`. | Scale repetitions immediately; pursue an end-to-end search redesign before locating the current bottleneck. | Researcher prioritized iteration efficiency; implementation and profile by Codex. | [Current status](../../documentation/current-status.md), [kernel report](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/forward-kernel-benchmark-2026-10-07/README.md), [matched cycle](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/learning-iteration-profile-signed-weights-2026-10-07/README.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:29:| 2026-10-07 | Treat the shared-memory multi-context run as an audited acquisition failure with a limited ranking response. | Independent audit passed 154 certificates, 96 panels and 24 recurrences. Both informative choices and fixed-budget searches failed, as did all controls; successful targets ended at ranks 11/32 and 5/30 only in the informative condition. | Call ranking movement a learned correct choice or transfer; discard evidence because top-1 choice failed. | Frozen protocol and run by Codex; interpretation follows predeclared all-choice gates. | [Run record](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/shared-memory-multi-context-coverage-v1-2026-10-07/README.md), [current status](../../documentation/current-status.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:37:| 2026-09-29 | Measure serial exact sparse gradients and propose a compute-budgeted criterion/changed-position curriculum. | Full released-graph engineering run used 130 MiB peak RAM; median update 0.648 s. This establishes kernel feasibility, not chess reasoning. | Batched GPU recipe; reservoir-only readout; zeroth-order updates; large self-play pipeline. | Assistant feasibility result and proposal; scientific protocol remains draft. | `design/low_memory_training.md`, `reports/lowram-feasibility.json` |
research/project/decision_log.md:38:| 2026-09-30 | Treat the rubric as feedback after experience; preserve released wiring and investigate endogenous learning mechanisms. | Researcher corrected the direct-target interpretation and objected to teacher-driven changes to the brain. A biological reward pathway, plasticity and memory have not been selected or validated. | Direct criterion-target supervision and external Adam/backpropagation; retained as baseline/engineering evidence. | Researcher clarified intent; mechanism remains unresolved. | [Design direction](../../documentation/design/research-goals/feedback-learning.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:43:| 2026-09-30 | Implement a bounded gamma4 direct-stimulation conditioning prototype, without connecting it to chess yet. | Handler 2019 supports opposite timing-dependent KC-to-MBON effects; pinned FlyWire identities and FlyBase ontology identify PAM08 and contralateral MBON05. Full-graph implementation checks pass with no-feedback and disabled-plasticity controls. Equation, time units and rates are unfitted assumptions; natural feedback, action choice and biological validation are absent. | Global generic reward updates; inventing reward neurons; claiming record-only search or engineered retention as biological learning. | Researcher requested implementation; specific reduced mechanism and protocol are assistant implementation choices, not researcher-approved scientific conclusions. | [Mechanism and limitations](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/gamma4-plasticity.md), [raw report](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/gamma4-conditioning-2026-09-30/report.json) |
research/project/decision_log.md:44:| 2026-09-30 | Test feedback-driven choices with frozen controls and memory ablations; retain failed acceptance and investigate experience coverage in a separate follow-up. | Researcher requested evidence of learning. V1 failed acquisition when three seeds received no acquisition rewards. V2 used six new seeds and target-blind initial sampling: acquisition effects positive in 6/6, reset/restoration causal, but reversed choices correct in only 3/6. Both overall acceptance tests failed; no rates or thresholds were tuned. | Treat any weight change, correct final choice, positive reversal margin or passing code test as complete learning validation; overwrite failed results. | Researcher requested validation; decoder, seed panels and opportunity-control follow-up are assistant experimental choices. | [Protocol and interpretation](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/gamma4-association-tests.md), [V1 report](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/association-validation-v1-2026-09-30/report.json), [V2 report](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/association-validation-v2-2026-09-30/report.json) |
research/project/decision_log.md:51:| 2026-10-05 | Test whether stored candidate memory reaches GF, retaining the proposed application's clipping failure. | Replayed 24 jobs reproduce original MBON values and causal GF reset/restoration; diagnostic informative choices are 6/6 in both phases. All cue signals are below rest, so application clipping yields ties and 3/6 per phase. Whole-model physiological adaptation remains an outstanding requirement. | Infer functional action learning from paths or an MBON score; silently replace the requested GF interface, tune offsets after outcomes, or reinterpret whole-model scope as local coverage. | Assistant prerequisite diagnostic; reused seeds and unvalidated physiology do not pass independent action/biological gates. | [Design/results](../../documentation/design/diagnostics/gf-readout-probe.md), [raw evidence](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/gf-memory-probe-v1-2026-10-05/README.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:52:| 2026-10-05 | Implement a bounded, teacher-label-free board/action input and freeze naive input-domain calibration before conditioning. | Ninety-two inputs produce distinct GF responses within each position; 91 response pairs globally. Four repeats and a tail resume reproduce exactly; graph/parameter hashes hold and 46 tests pass. New calibration retains differences but ties, saturation, choice polarity and learned-state behavior remain unresolved. | Encode puzzle answers/IDs, train a translator on good moves, overwrite earlier clipping failures, or equate input diversity with learning/reasoning. | Assistant implementation of the requested input boundary; direct KC stimulation and GF scoring remain engineered, unvalidated interfaces. | [Input design](../../documentation/design/chess-input/board-action-cues.md), [raw panel](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/chess-cue-panel-v1-2026-10-05/README.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:53:| 2026-10-05 | Test choice adaptation on overlapping board/action cues with new encoder seeds; retain failed acquisition/reversal. | Frozen six-seed/four-control experiment fails overall: 3/6 acquired/reversed GF choices, none changes from baseline. All 384 local updates audit correctly; causal memory, controls and fixed state pass. Signed GF diagnostics do not fix choice counts; MBON diagnostics cannot replace the specified decoder. | Turn positive margins or passed consistency audits into a learning pass; fabricate reversed chess truth, retune gates or silently substitute readouts. | Assistant prerequisite test under the requested objective; experimental punishment labels are not verified chess feedback or biological validation. | [Experiment design](../../documentation/design/chess-input/board-action-association.md), [raw failure and diagnostics](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/chess-cue-association-v1-2026-10-05/README.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:54:| 2026-10-05 | Test whether removing shared stored efficacy after learning rescues failed GF choices. | Sixty intact/unique/shared/other/naive interventions produce no choice changes; all masks/readouts audit and intact/naive responses reproduce exactly. This does not rule out interference during acquisition. | Convert a diagnostic reset into a biological learning rule; infer a rescue or a complete causal explanation from changed rates; override source failure. | Assistant causal diagnosis within the main research goal; no new learning or biological acceptance. | [Ablation design](../../documentation/design/diagnostics/memory-ablation.md), [raw evidence](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/chess-memory-ablation-v1-2026-10-05/README.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:55:| 2026-10-06 | Review fixed efficacy-floor sensitivity and retain failed learner status. | No decoded or signed choice changes under 32 nonempty unilateral profiles; four empty masks are controls, not intervention evidence. Masks/baselines and one-profile resume audit exactly; 46 tests pass. | Treat engineering bounds as physiological limits, infer universal inability from selected profiles, or claim completed learning from diagnostics. | Assistant diagnostic review; researcher still requests useful puzzle learning and reasoning/transfer, with no validated replacement mechanism yet. | [Sensitivity design](../../documentation/design/diagnostics/efficacy-floor.md), [raw evidence](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/chess-memory-envelope-v1-2026-10-05/README.md), [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-10-06-learning-experiments.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:58:| 2026-10-06 | Accept MBON-choice V1 only as a passed computational prerequisite; retain unfinished puzzle/biological/transfer scope. | All strict choice/ranking gates pass 6/6; both phase effects exceed absent/shuffled controls; causal memory/control/fixed-state gates pass. All 384 updates and two-event prefix audit exactly. Old-action-specific recovery observed 6/6; same-seed GF remains 4/6 acquisition and 3/6 reversal. Audit-only heap failure repaired by streaming and preserved in raw record. | Promote an engineered MBON subtraction to natural action control, claim chess learning or whole-model reasoning, erase old failures or conceal the audit memory failure. | Researcher-proposed solution tested without changing encoder/plasticity/dynamics or fitting coefficients. | [Completed raw evidence](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/mbon-choice-v1-2026-10-06/README.md), [design](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/mbon-choice.md) |
research/project/decision_log.md:62:| 2026-10-06 | Add a separate candidate-relative input representation and audit a read-only full-graph panel; clarify absence of eyes-based perception. | Fifty-two exact symmetry pairs, four checked outcome contrasts, full legal coverage/repeats/resume and 55 tests pass. Overlap falls but cell count/current differs; no learning conclusion. V1 castling-colour ordering limitation preserved. Both encoders directly stimulate KCs from structured input. | Claim engineered invariance as learned reasoning, hide dose differences, silently replace old encoder or imply graph presence reconstructs vision. | Assistant input redesign toward researcher reasoning goal; researcher asked about eyes, no visual-path implementation requested or claimed. | [Design and eyes boundary](../../documentation/design/chess-input/relational-encoder.md), [raw panel](../../archive/early-development/research/reports/relational-cue-panel-v1-2026-10-06/README.md) |
