research/project/decision_log.md:1736:All six conditions and the separate arithmetic audit complete. Fly+memory, memory-only readout and Fly-only each finish with all four associations correct at acquisition, short delay and reversal. The two memory-backed systems make 23/24 correct reversal choices versus Fly-only 12/24 on this seed; fixed score calibration/exploration and different chosen feedback histories remain part of that descriptive difference. Combined absent/shuffled/disabled controls fail the joint pilot criterion. The combined world's raw neural readout still chooses the old reversed action; external recall supplies the correct override. Retain long-term-memory v1 as an integrated engineering component, not a selected best architecture or evidence of reasoning. The prior failure protocol was not reproduced exactly, and this easier/changed protocol gives no final-accuracy advantage. [Complete report and audit](../reports/long-term-memory-v1-live-comparison-2026-11-01/README.md). Next scientific question: unfamiliar combinations and multiple predeclared seeds against the same lookup-only comparator, not retuning this pilot for a winner.
research/project/decision_log.md:1741:Completed the previously authorized two-sign arbitrary-conditioning pilot in serial, using frozen baseline stimuli, all controls and separate arithmetic/replay. Repaired isolated pilot diagnostics/audit compatibility, not a proven biological component. Full-graph passive-reader contract passes. Retain sign alternatives and leave chooser/complete eye test blocked by F04; do not substitute a 1.0 corner for the frozen 1.8 gate. F03 replacement still needs real-history generator acceptance. Preserve engineered memory, released graph and all prior evidence. [Before/after sources, failures, audits and one next experiment](../reports/overnight-eyes-reader-repair-2026-11-01/REPORT.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1745:Completed the requested self-performed scope/protocol review. The earlier mechanism-first recommendation was not researcher approval; recommend reasoning-first engineered representation/memory work, with mechanism-first as an explicit alternative. Biological-memory repair is not a Volume 3 prerequisite. Exact-key memory has no cross-key inference; propose a distinct similarity condition and matched raw/random/rewired controllers. Correct the overstrong linear-KC-access interpretation in the historical F04 draft; propose scoped attainable interventions or a separate hybrid route, preserving original gates and failures. [Reviewable scope and source coverage](../reports/elife-prospective-scope-2026-11-02/README.md), [mistakes/pivots and before/after links](../journal/discussions/2026-11-02-elife-scope-and-protocol.md). Researcher choices on scope, mechanism, F03/F04 and budget remain explicit. No agents, graph/training, sealed access, protected-source edits or publication.
research/project/decision_log.md:1750:The solo research window produced [exact root/edge provenance and a shared-baseline contradiction](../reports/interference-limits-2026-11-02/README.md) from saved responses, independently checked with rational arithmetic. Reject the stronger all-state strict-ranking impossibility: the existing six-tuple restoration is a static feasible counterexample. Endogenous reachability and joint gate feasibility remain open. A64-mask causal localization is prospective only; no memory mechanism, gate amendment or graph experiment adopted. Exact model-specific limits are a separate potential contribution, while unfamiliar concept combination remains the primary aim. [Mistakes and pivot record](../journal/experiments/2026-11-02-interference-limits-and-certificates.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1754:Researcher directs four GPT-6.1 Sol medium reviewers, two school/two primary, and parent synthesis of problems only. Keep the question whether the fly-derived system reasons under stated constraints; do not substitute a biological-memory repair project. Four reviews and bounded parent checks produce eight distinct issues. No code fix, gate amendment or learner run is authorized by the report. Preserve pilot sources, failures and results; later work must address the specific observation, audit coverage, launch-control and inference contracts before reusing affected paths. [Ranked evidence and closure requirements](../reports/overnight-peer-review-2026-11-02/ranked-problems.md); [mistake journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-02-overnight-peer-review.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1758:The researcher directed fixes for every confirmed problem in the 2026-11-02 review. Decisions: new tests use Eyes v2 (captured piece shown at brightness 0.25) and every task set must pass the input-collision check; the pilot audit requires complete answer lists; the label `check` no longer skips safety checks before training starts for brain-model commands; every future chooser scores all moves from unchanged memory (`frozenScores`); restart plan section 7 stays preserved but blocked, and Eye test v2 uses strengths 0.2 and 1.0 with an exact strength measure and the original count rules; the training move score is MBON05 at −1, switched on only after the separately approved memory test; the en-passant group becomes an en-passant capture by the fly; the separate answer file is a comparison system only, joined by similar-board recall; Phases B and C have complete tasks, arms, pass scores and stopping rules, with Phase C split into two ideas and then three. No frozen number changed, no training ran and no sealed data was opened. [Decisions](../../documentation/design/research-goals/restart-plan-revision-2026-11-03.md), [protocol](../../documentation/design/research-goals/transfer-and-combination-protocol-2026-11-03.md), [fix report](../reports/overnight-peer-review-2026-11-02/fixes-2026-11-03.md), [mistakes and pivots](../journal/experiments/2026-11-03-peer-review-fixes.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1762:The researcher asked to test whether the fly can see the board. Proposal C data (old-game exclusion verified, 300 training-bucket histories fetched, 136 twin pairs generated) and one Eye test v2 run followed. Outcome: pipeline fault (tokens 41/48 < 44); raw-eyes shortcut (38/48); Graph 26/48; strength ratio 0.04%. By the frozen rule the run does not count, and no rendering or threshold change is made without researcher approval. The memory formula and memory test remain unapproved. [Result](../reports/eye-test-v2-2026-11-03/README.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1764:## 2026-11-03 — Seeing test v1: the image reaches the memory cells
research/project/decision_log.md:1768:## 2026-11-03 — Memory test v1 failed; engineered memory next (build only)
research/project/decision_log.md:1770:The γ4 ReversalMemory test (12 arms) gave identical results in every arm, including learning off; the reversal failed everywhere. Design mistakes are recorded: the rewarded candidate matched the built-in preference, and visual input drives PAM08, so the no-feedback arm still updated. The MBON05 contract stays switched off. The researcher's standing instruction on failure: design and build, but do not run, the disclosed engineered-memory condition. [Result](../reports/memory-test-2026-11-03/README.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1772:## 2026-11-03 — Engineered memory: v1 replaced by v2 (built, not run)
research/project/decision_log.md:1774:Review of the first engineered-memory build (v1, never run) found it scored only seen boards, let the oldest of equally similar patterns win (so its reversal could never pass), used a random projection of the brain's own Kenyon-cell activity as its "random" control, and did not require beating raw or random features. v2 follows the problem 7 correction and the eLife protocol option 2: certified safe-capture twin pairs (training bucket), 96 practice and 38 new-board pairs, nearest-neighbour store over Kenyon-cell activity, compared at the same practice with raw eyes, random sparse features, mismatched outcomes and no memory. Changes from the eLife proposal: capacity 256 and 1/distance weighting. Lessons from memory test v1 applied: balanced correct slot and a no-memory arm that reads and writes nothing. Rewired graph not included. Pass rule fixed before any run. No brain run, training or sealed data. [Design](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/engineered-memory-test-v2.md), [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-03-peer-review-fixes.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1776:## 2026-11-03 — Engineered memory v2 run, diagnosis, and v3 (episodic memory without hints)
research/project/decision_log.md:1778:The researcher asked to run the memory test, find exactly where it fails, fix it if possible, delete the redundant item and design memory v3 without hints. v2 ran once and did not pass (graph 20-22/38 new pairs). A read-only pathway trace located the failure: the Kenyon-cell code shrinks the deciding one-piece difference 48 times, and whole-board similarity retrieval fails even on eye input with perfect labels. Practice-only fix attempts did not help the Kenyon-cell code, so no memory change was adopted. The test design faults (twin hint, brightness shortcut, small practice) are removed in v3: real positions, all legal moves scored, feedback on the chosen move only, one pass, same comparison arms. v3 is built and registered paused. The stale benchmark lock was deleted; v1 files are kept as history. [v2 result and trace](../reports/engineered-memory-test-v2-2026-11-03/README.md), [v3 design](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/engineered-memory-test-v3.md), [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-03-peer-review-fixes.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1783:The researcher requested a proposal addressing the unchanged failed matching method and limited successful practice in memory v3. The training-data-only uniform-choice reference gives about 15.34 positive events over 388 positions. Code inspection finds that the current tied-top-score rule makes the no-memory comparison automatically score zero. A [new proposal](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/memory-v3-review-proposal-2026-11-04.md) recommends review, a smaller fixed-budget development screen with repeated scheduled practice and exact experience replay across comparisons, consistent seeded tie choices, and explicit positive-experience coverage before scaling. All numerical choices remain proposals. The existing v3 code, pinned protocol, pause and results are unchanged; no experiment was authorized or run. [Journal and evidence limits](../journal/experiments/2026-11-04-memory-v3-review-proposal.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1787:The researcher confirmed the chapter names and chapter content for all five volumes. New chapter files replace the 2026-11-01 working templates, which are kept in a superseded-outline folder in each volume. Volume 3 tests the connectome with disclosed aids and reports either outcome; Volume 4 proves a bounded limit of the raw connectome model, not the living fly. Volume 5 targets a Capablanca playing style and an open, versioned release anyone can play. No experiment was run. [Series plan](../../documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md), [journal](../journal/discussions/2026-11-04-confirmed-chapter-names.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1793:## 2026-11-04 - Memory v3 run, diagnosis, and memory v4
research/project/decision_log.md:1795:The researcher asked to continue testing the fly with more CPU, then diagnose the mistakes with Codex, research fixes, design a new proposal, have it approved by a strict Codex peer reviewer, and build it with Haiku agents. Memory v3 ran at duty 1 (runner change and direct launch recorded; first launch ran out of JS heap and is preserved) and did not pass: ties and unequal exposure decided most scores. Codex ranked 32 mistakes. Pivot: memory v4, an externally scheduled representation assay (identical verified ledger for every arm, s / c tie scoring, numeric bootstrap gate, sparse and centred KC transforms with matched shuffled controls, full certificates, replayable cache and an independent audit). Two review rounds; building allowed. Built, unit tested, dry-run and audited. Not launched: needs researcher approval as its own condition. The launcher now accepts duty 1. [v3 result](../reports/engineered-memory-test-v3-2026-11-04/README.md), [diagnosis and reviews](../reports/mistake-diagnosis-2026-11-04/README.md), [v4 proposal](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/memory-v4-proposal-2026-11-04.md), [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-04-memory-v3-run.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1797:## 2026-11-04 (night) - Researcher goal and permissions for overnight memory work
research/project/decision_log.md:1799:The researcher set a standing goal: "repeat until the memory is solved", with permission to act and full CPU (laptop plugged in, ventilated). Answers given before the night: (1) done means "a memory that supports learning, so that it can learn useful memories that will be stored as experience"; (2) new versions (v5, v6, ...) may be designed and run overnight, each approved by the strict Codex reviewer before launch; (3) a trained reader fitted on practice data only (for example a linear readout over saved Kenyon-cell activity) is allowed as a clearly labeled engineered aid, kept out of any brain-only claim. Frozen gates of finished or registered experiments stay unchanged; new gates are written before each run.
research/project/decision_log.md:1803:Under the researcher's standing memory goal: v4 (fixed-schedule representation assay) did not pass, but raw and random keys learned, while the fly's KC keys barely did (audited 8/8). v5 selected a position-centred KC key in practice-only development. It beat no memory and (in 2 of 3 seeds) shuffled outcomes on 150 fresh positions, but did not show improvement with more experience: does not pass. Pivot: v6, a frozen replication of v5 on 573 positions from 300 newly fetched games. It was stopped at 6 of 24 jobs for a laptop shutdown; its partial seed 6101 clears every margin, but this is not evidence until the rerun. Reviews, reports and journal: `research/reports/mistake-diagnosis-2026-11-04/`, `research/reports/engineered-memory-test-v4-2026-11-05/`, `research/reports/engineered-memory-test-v5-2026-11-05/`, `research/journal/experiments/2026-11-05-memory-v4-v5.md`.
research/project/decision_log.md:1805:## 2026-11-05 - Missing random-capture baseline; memory v7 drafted
research/project/decision_log.md:1807:Found after the v6 relaunch, at the researcher's question about clues: no answer reaches the choice, but the v3-v6 gate scores over all legal moves while the store marks every quiet move −1, so learning capture-versus-quiet alone can pass it. A random capture scores 250.1 on the v6 test; the stopped v6 seed-6101 full arm scored 130.9. Decision: v6 keeps running unchanged (frozen gate; it still answers full − quarter), and its report adds the random-capture and captures-only scores as post-hoc descriptions. Memory v7 is drafted to score among known-certificate captures only, on new games 601-900, and goes to strict review before any build. [Journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-05-memory-v4-v5.md); [v7 proposal](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/memory-v7-proposal-2026-11-05.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1811:v6-r3 failed at the 5-h wall guard (21/24 jobs; the limit was copied from v5's smaller test). Decision, reviewed by Codex (LAUNCH OK): raise the wall to 12 h in the v6 and v7 runners and resume into v6-r4 (rebuild inputs, check their hashes equal r3, copy the 21 finished outputs, run 3 jobs; the audit rebuilds all 24). v6-r4 **passes** `judgeV6` in all three seeds, including full > quarter (+43 to +52, bounds 23-34), with 0/749 memories lost. Interpretation is limited by the random-capture rule (250.1 vs 131-145): v6 shows learning that grows with experience, not safe-capture judgement. v7 (captures only) started 13:18; the v7, v6 and v5 audits are queued after it. A v7 dry run was lost to a session exit; every launch is now detached. [Report](../reports/engineered-memory-test-v6-2026-11-06/README.md); [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-05-memory-v4-v5.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1815:v7 failed in one seed with about 300 capture experiences per seed. Decision (Codex: approve with changes, then LAUNCH OK): v8 stores every known-certificate capture of the 750 practice positions once (2,509; a joint curriculum change, disclosed), key, memory and gate unchanged, new test games 901-1200. v8 **passes** `judgeV8` in all three seeds: full 349.3 vs none 233.8 and shuffled 208-242; full > quarter by about 31 (bounds 4.3-6.4); 0/627 lost; it also beats the piece-value rule (294.7). Raw input (383) and random features (363) beat the fly's key. The full-run audit was missing from the queue; it was added at 05:00 and moved before the v6-r4 audit. If the audit passes, the written memory goal is met by v8. [Report](../reports/engineered-memory-test-v8-2026-11-07/README.md); [journal](../journal/experiments/2026-11-05-memory-v4-v5.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1819:The independent v8 audit passed and is pinned (10/10, 06:13). v8 therefore meets all five conditions of the memory goal set 2026-11-04. Decision: the memory goal iteration stops here; v9 is not started. Stronger claims are separate work that needs the researcher's direction: an untouched confirmatory replication, a fly key that beats random features, and all-legal choice without the capture filter. The v6-r4 and v5 audits continue detached. [Report](../reports/engineered-memory-test-v8-2026-11-07/README.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1823:After v8 met the 2026-11-04 goal, the researcher's questions showed that the goal did not match their claims. v8 uses a filtered choice (all-legal score 30.6 against random 21.9 of 560), an engineered store that cannot overwrite, a fly key below random features, and feedback written into the store. The goal also put an engineered memory in Volume 2, against the 2026-11-01 decision that placed it in Volume 3. Decision: the [memory handoff](../../documentation/design/research-goals/memory-handoff-2026-11-07.md) defines the requirement (P1 fly connections, P2 lasts during new learning, P3 new positions without clues over all legal moves, P4 lessons for Volume 3) and the rules for new memory work. The 2026-11-04 goal and all results stay as history. The next test (P2 in chess with the fly's own connections) needs researcher approval. [Discussion and mistakes](../journal/discussions/2026-11-07-memory-claim-gap.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1827:The researcher supplied the [memory goal prompt](../../documentation/design/research-goals/memory-goal-prompt-2026-11-07.md), authorizing design, strict review, Haiku builds, detached dry/full runs and audits with full CPU for P1-P3. This replaces the approval wait, not the scientific gates. Start from the sourced interference-mechanism decision and inspect the completed APL feasibility work before proposing a repair. No filter, answer store or changed frozen gate can satisfy the primary claim. P4 is outside scope. Live-process inspection finds no graph/audit work; the old v5 audit is unfinished and is not restarted. [Evidence and inspection mistakes](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1831:Strict Codex review returned [SOURCE NO-GO](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/design-review-r1-retry.md): the proposed cue-only/cue-plus-teaching subtraction creates an unsourced extra-circuit signal. Local APL also changes total inhibition, the Gkanias circuit names PAM07 rather than the draft's PAM08, and the P2/seed/workload definitions have confounds. Decision: preserve and do not build that draft; revise source applicability and actual local teaching before another review. This does not prove a biological limit or justify external primary memory. Separately, exact Huang author code was acquired and hash-checked; its effective signed weights and assay-level last-training switch make direct transplantation inappropriate. [Source coverage](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/huang-source-applicability.md); [mistakes and next step](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1835:Decision: replace the rejected counterfactual teaching variable with actual simultaneous KC/PAM07 model activity and the source's negative DPR branch, retaining all endogenous activity; absent is no external pulse, and learning-off is the no-update control. This remains a candidate requiring source review. Add a strength-matched APL comparison, operational effective-write pressure, successful acquired-choice retention, separately identified contingency-reversal probes, actual seed-dependent tapes and finite pass accounting. Static annotation/NPZ checks confirm 18 PAM07 roots and matching graph/annotation/ontology pins. [Revision 2](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-revision-2-2026-11-07.md) is under strict review; no code or graph launch follows until explicitly allowed. [Record of changes and inspection error](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1839:Strict Codex high-effort review round 2 returns [BUILD ALLOWED](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/design-review-r2.md). Decision: proceed with the isolated implementation, initially two scoped Haiku xhigh module/fixture tasks, preserving the rejected draft and all frozen sources. This is sufficient source justification for a computational experiment, not physiological validation or launch permission. Keep control interpretation and the original one-run/all-seed P1-P3 completion contract. [Build state, remaining pipeline and logging-order correction](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1841:## 2026-11-08 - Fly connections memory v1: built, code review r1, scope changes before data
research/project/decision_log.md:1843:Wave-2/3 pipeline (packet, condition, baselines, job loop, runner, judge, auditor) built by Haiku agents and reviewed; 125/125 tests. Strict Codex code review r1: LAUNCH OK WITH REQUIRED CHANGES. Decisions: add own-baseline gate P2.2b (closing a red criterion in my gates file); withdraw the off-arm ranking reuse; authenticate the PAM07/MBON05 mapping independently and pin it; make the auditor's bootstrap independent. Declared scope changes from revision 2, before any data: no v8-style KC-key store; primary midpoint APL geometry only; test boards are task-selected. Sealed test boards come from 300 fresh development-bucket games. [Journal items 11-14](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md); [gates](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-gates-2026-11-08.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1845:## 2026-11-09 - Fly connections memory v1: dry run fails on mechanism; stop for researcher decision
research/project/decision_log.md:1847:Dry run (development data) completed: P1 checks pass (pulse reaches PAM07 and changes only mask synapses), every P2/P3 gate fails (0/8 A, 0/8 B acquired; test success equals learning-off). Trace: all 2,096 gamma KCs tonically active; moves on one board share about 97% of their KC pattern; learning shifts all moves together. Codex diagnosis: STOP FOR RESEARCHER DECISION. Decision: do not launch the 96-CPU-hour full run until the researcher chooses between (a) running it as registered for a confirmed negative result on this exact model, and (b) a new, source-bounded redesign (KC sparsening or visual gamma-d KCs), which is a new version with new review. Before either, split gate P2.2 by group (red criterion). [Journal items 16-17](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md); [diagnosis](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/diagnosis-r1.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1849:## 2026-11-09 - Fly connections memory v1 stopped for a source-less calibration
research/project/decision_log.md:1851:Diagnostics show the source rule cannot act in this rate model without choosing a tonic drive level and a KC activity scale that no source provides. Stopped under the goal's stop clause; [stop report](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/stop-report-2026-11-09.md). The sealed development-bucket test games remain unused.
research/project/decision_log.md:1855:After the predeclared single-shot capacity gate failed, a sequential exact search found safe-capture settings on all three development boards. Because this method was chosen after seeing the gate failure, the original gate remains failed until an independent review assesses the new evidence. The subsequent reward-only and valence probes left the safe capture below its best rival on all three boards; the valence probe was one arm with no matched no-pulse control or independent audit. [Journal items 29-31](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md); [updated stop report](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/stop-report-2026-11-09.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1869:Proposal, not yet approved. A replay regenerated the zero-drive cell readings that journal item 25 had used. At scale 0.014047, capping keeps at least 99% of the activity difference for every pair of legal moves on the three development boards. Only one cell on one move per board reaches the cap. The proposed test compares the current circuit with a quiet, rescaled circuit (zero drive, `k = min(1, rate / 0.014047)`, dopamine formula unchanged). It uses correct, swapped-label and learning-off conditions, fresh memory per board, and fixed rules for expanding to 12 boards, dropping the approach, or calling the result inconclusive. Corrections found on the way: item 26's drive labels were swapped; items 30–31 carried learned changes across boards; item 31's dopamine summary mixed rewards and punishments. Evidence: [protocol](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/three-board-feedback-test-2026-11-14.md), [scale check](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/calibration-replay-2026-11-14/README.md), [journal items 33–34](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md#33-2026-11-14-three-board-feedback-test-protocol-and-corrections-found).
research/project/decision_log.md:1875:Built, not run. `probe-wide-learning-v3.mjs`, a shared rules module, a rules script and 16 tests implement the [protocol](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/three-board-feedback-test-2026-11-14.md). The decision rules changed before any data. Any failed check, or fewer than 2 boards eligible in both circuits, now gives inconclusive first, then expand, then drop, then inconclusive. Before this change, one result could satisfy both "drop" and "inconclusive". The cutoff numbers are unchanged. Correction to the entry above: the cap is reached on two moves in total, across two boards, not "one move per board". The copied scale checker's import was repointed so it runs from the report folder. Codex reports that the full 6,046-connection recheck keeps at least 99%, and that all three boards clear the starting-gap cutoff in both circuits. Evidence: [journal item 35](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md#35-2026-11-14-three-board-feedback-test-built-rules-made-single-answer), [scale check](../reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/calibration-replay-2026-11-14/README.md).
research/project/decision_log.md:1881:Codex's review of `5c0f6fe3` found two bugs in the rules code; both are fixed before any data. Values exactly on a cutoff are now judged as written: exactly 20% or 20 or 10 points meets the requirement, and exactly 5 points is not "under 5". Before, floating-point rounding could flip them. The cutoff numbers are unchanged. Lesson records are now checked against the verified board packet, because a run with six lessons, or with a duplicate hiding a missing lesson, could trigger "expand". Evidence: [journal item 36](../journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md#36-2026-11-14-two-bugs-in-the-tests-rules-code-fixed-after-codexs-review), [protocol](../../documentation/design/neural-learning/three-board-feedback-test-2026-11-14.md).
32:1. The four points above are now the test for **one learned concept**. They are necessary, but one concept is not reasoning. Phase A alone can never support a reasoning claim.
46:   - Code still supplies the list of legal moves. This hides some legality information from the task, so the Fly never has to reject an illegal move.
175:**Phase A (principle):** train with own-choice feedback on training-pool puzzles. Choose the snapshot using a separate development set only. Then freeze everything and run the locked test.
177:**Phase B (the thesis):** only if Phase A passes. Before Phase B runs, you approve one addendum with a new task and its own locked set. Compare, at equal practice: Fly with Phase A memory; Fly starting fresh; Fly with Phase A exposure but shuffled feedback. **The thesis is supported only if the Phase A memory Fly beats both others on the new task's locked set.** If not, we report that the thesis failed in this test.
187:## 9. Pass and fail rules for Phase A
194:4. **Real choice:** among all legal moves, the Fly picks a safe capture on more boards than each arm, with the same bootstrap limit above 0.
206:"EUREKA EUREKA" stays reserved until these gates, the Phase C gates and the independent audit pass. Correction 2026-10-30: the first draft reserved it for Phase A only, which the researcher's definition does not support.
